  {
   "id": "sZHMBMjFAQAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2017-09-10T10:40:47Z",
   "subject": "Re: intercept differences between MLM and WLSMV in MI model",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>So, why is that? Is this to be expected since ML is not appropriate for ordinal data? Or are the models more different than i think? </div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I would try asking about this on SEMNET, since it is not about using lavaan (you would have the same question if your results were from Mplus).</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792426734509000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1bGJ4WMyXUfCwDrHwSYAbq\">http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>But I think you are right, the models are more different than you think.  With DWLS, you constrained all intercepts to be zero, and thresholds were estimated relative to that assumed location of the latent item-response distribution.  With ML, you estimated intercepts, so they were nonzero (and equal across groups).  So the location (and scale) of the latent item-response distributions are different under DWLS than the distributions of the observed &quot;continuous&quot; indicators under ML, and that has (perhaps complicated) implications for the estimated common factor that has an assumed linear effect on those latent item-response distributions.</div><div><br></div><div>As a point of curiosity, you could simply try fixing the intercepts in the DWLS model to the estimated values from the ML model.  The model would be statistically equivalent, but thresholds would be estimated relative to those locations, which would in turn affect the latent means.  Might help you think about the problem, but no guarantee that the result would agree with your intuition any more than the current result does.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">UvA web page: <a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792426734509000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2W7jXC0P8S24O2XU0kCq-G\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
